"Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>Membrane Diffusion: Flippases</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp><p:sp><p:nvSpPr><p:cNvPr id="455" name="Google Shape;455;p62"/><p:cNvSpPr txBox="1"/><p:nvPr/></p:nvSpPr><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="381000" y="762000"/><a:ext cx="7772400" cy="2667000"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln><a:noFill/></a:ln></p:spPr><p:txBody><a:bodyPr anchorCtr="0" anchor="t" bIns="45700" lIns="91425" spcFirstLastPara="1" rIns="91425" wrap="square" tIns="45700"><a:noAutofit/></a:bodyPr><a:lstStyle/><a:p><a:pPr indent="-342900" lvl="0" marL="342900" marR="0" rtl="0" algn="l"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="100000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2800"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="•"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2800" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>Special enzymes catalyze transverse diffusion.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr indent="-285750" lvl="1" marL="742950" marR="0" rtl="0" algn="l"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="100000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="480"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2400"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="–"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2400" u="none" cap="none" strike="noStrike"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>Though often referred to by category name “flippase,” there are unique unidirectional and bidirectional enzymes to catalyze lipid movement.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr indent="-342900" lvl="0" marL="342900" marR="0" rtl="0" algn="l"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="100000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="560"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2800"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="•"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2800" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>Some flippases use energy of ATP to move lipids against the concentration gradient.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp><p:cxnSp><p:nvCxnSpPr><p:cNvPr id="456" name="Google Shape;456;p62"/><p:cNvCxnSpPr/><p:nvPr/></p:nvCxnSpPr><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="457200" y="762000"/><a:ext cx="8229600" cy="0"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="straightConnector1"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln cap="flat" cmpd="thinThick" w="57150"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="2FB0DC"/></a:solidFill><a:prstDash val="solid"/><a:miter lim="800000"/><a:headEnd len="med" w="med" type="none"/><a:tailEnd len="med" w="med" type="none"/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:cxnSp><p:pic><p:nvPicPr><p:cNvPr descr="An illustration showing catalyzed transbilayer translocations (labeled above). This time, the red shaded unit is on the left end of the membrane, but has an NH3+ bonded to its head. To the right of the unit is a huge pink translocater protein. It is long and cylindrical and spans the membrane, with a bulbous bottom end. A dashed pink arrow curves down and right from the red lipid unit through the left side of the translocater. The arrow points to the new location of the lipid unit, which is “flipped” to the bottom layer of the membrane, head down. A curved arrow at the bottom is labeled ATP on the left end and ADP + Pi on the right end, indicating use of ATP. This is labeled at the bottom, “(P-type ATPase) moves PE and PS from outer to cytosolic leaflet.” In the image to the right, the red shaded lipid unit flips upward to the top layer of the membrane via the large pink protein to its right. A dashed pink arrow curves up and left to show the direction of movement through the protein. Below the image is labeled, “Floppase (ABC transporter) moves phospholipids from cytosolic to outer leaflet.” To the right of this image, the red shaded lipid unit is shown on both sides of the translocater protein, the left unit part of the bottom layer (head down) and the right unit part of the top layer (head up). 2 curved arrows curve in opposite directions, showing that the protein can move lipids either up or down. Ca2+ is labeled near the bottom of the protein. The image is labeled below, “Scramblase moves lipids in either direction, toward equilibrium.”" id="457" name="Google Shape;457;p62"/><p:cNvPicPr preferRelativeResize="0"/><p:nvPr/></p:nvPicPr><p:blipFill rotWithShape="1"><a:blip r:embed="rId3"><a:alphaModFix/></a:blip><a:srcRect b="0" l="0" r="0" t="0"/><a:stretch/></p:blipFill><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="2546350" y="3429000"/><a:ext cx="4159250" cy="3357562"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln><a:noFill/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:pic></p:spTree></p:cSld><p:clrMapOvr><a:masterClrMapping/></p:clrMapOvr></p:sld>